# BUGS

A subgroup reduction or scan costs 16 permutes; vector 8 and 16-bit arithmetic is scalarised, not paired in the half-register forms.
Derivatives in a compute shader, of 16-bit floats and of a cube image's explicit gradients are refused; the spec leaves open what the cube's four derivative words mean (12b.5.8).
A fragment shader's Layer is read from the frame argument, where the driver must write it; a vertex shader's PointSize is accepted and not stored.
Matrices, ClipDistance and CullDistance, geometry and tessellation, subgroup operations at a scope other than Subgroup, group operations other than Reduce, InclusiveScan, ExclusiveScan and ClusteredReduce, and OpFRem and OpFMod are refused.
Functions are not inlined by the compiler; a function with a vector or aggregate parameter or result is refused by the scalarisation or the lowering (the Valhall backend inlines first).
Arrays of buffers are refused.

## Storage images

OpImageRead, OpImageWrite, OpImageTexelPointer and the queries OpImageQuerySize, OpImageQueryLevels and OpImageQuerySamples on a 1D or 2D storage image (arrayed or not) of a declared format, from a UniformConstant variable as above.
The format gives the channels (1 to 4) and whether a texel is float (also the normalised formats), unsigned or signed; the 32-bit texel types of the SPIR-V format are accepted, and the format Unknown is refused, since the access does not say what the descriptor holds.
OpImageRead becomes one `imgload` for each channel the format has (a missing one reads 0, 0, 1 for the green, blue and alpha it lacks); OpImageWrite becomes one `imgstore` of the channels the format has; OpImageTexelPointer, on a single-channel integer format, becomes an `imgaddr`, a `ptr<global>` the atomics use.
They take the element index, the i32 coordinates and the layer, and `ImgFlags` in `imm` says dimensions, arrayed, channels and kind.
An access outside the image reads zeros and writes nothing.
Capabilities StorageImageExtendedFormats, ImageQuery, StorageImageArrayDynamicIndexing and StorageImageArrayNonUniformIndexing are accepted.
The queries become arithmetic on `descword` values, words of the image's descriptor (a null descriptor gives 0).
Image operands (but SignExtend and ZeroExtend, which a 32-bit texel has nothing to extend by), 3D, cube, multisampled and depth storage images are refused by name.

A texel buffer is the same accesses with one coordinate, the element index, and `ImgFlags.buffer` set: OpImageFetch of a sampled buffer image (a samplerBuffer; the format is the descriptor's, so the access reads four channels of the kind the sampled type is, and nothing else of a buffer is read: a sample, a gather and image operands are refused), and OpImageRead, OpImageWrite and OpImageTexelPointer of a storage buffer image of a declared format.
Capabilities SampledBuffer, ImageBuffer, and UniformTexelBufferArrayNonUniformIndexing and StorageTexelBufferArrayNonUniformIndexing are accepted.
The size of a buffer image (OpImageQuerySize) is the descriptor's size over its stride (words 1 and 4, an unsigned divide), 0 for a null descriptor; its levels and samples are not asked of a buffer.
A texel-buffer fetch takes the image operands SignExtend and ZeroExtend, which change nothing, as a storage image's read does.

## Images and samplers

A UniformConstant variable of an image, sampler or array of them, with a DescriptorSet and a Binding, is followed at compile time, not held in a register: OpLoad, OpAccessChain into an array (one index; a NonUniform index is an ordinary one) and OpSampledImage and OpImage only pass its set, binding and element index along.
OpImageSampleImplicitLod, OpImageSampleExplicitLod, their Dref and Proj forms (a projective depth compare excepted), OpImageFetch, OpImageGather and OpImageDrefGather become `tex` instructions, one for each component of the texel (a Dref sample has one).
A `tex` has the element index of the image and of the sampler (i32), the coordinates (f32 for a sample, i32 for a fetch; for a cube image the three of the direction), the layer when the image is arrayed (a float for a sample, rounded to the nearest, halves up), the depth reference (f32) when the access compares, then an offset word (three signed bytes; for a fetch the level in byte 3 and for a multisampled fetch the sample in byte 2) and a level word (the bias or the explicit level as 8.8 fixed point) when the flags say so, then, for explicit gradients, the derivatives with respect to x and then to y.
A projection divides the coordinates by the last component in the IR.
A gather's channels are the hardware's footprint order, and the lowering permutes them into the API's; ConstOffsets are four gathers, each giving one channel.
OpImageQuerySizeLod, OpImageQuerySize, OpImageQueryLevels and OpImageQuerySamples of a sampled image are arithmetic on `descword` values (a level above 0 halves the base size, to a minimum of one; a cube array's count is the descriptor's cubes times six), and OpImageQueryLod is a `tex` that gives the level of detail as 8.8 fixed point, clamped in arithmetic to the sampler's limits (read with `descword` from the sampler's descriptor) and the image's levels and adjusted for the sampler's mipmap mode.
It is not pure: a computed level needs the lanes of its quad, so no pass moves it across control flow.
The image operands Bias, Lod, Grad, ConstOffset, Offset (a value computed at run time), ConstOffsets and Sample are lowered; an offset must fit a signed byte.
A coordinate may have more components than the access needs (a depth reference is carried in the extra one): they are ignored.
OpDPdx, OpDPdy and OpFwidth, with their Fine and Coarse forms, in a fragment shader, become `deriv` instructions, one for each component (OpDPdx and OpDPdy are the coarse ones, and a Fwidth is the sum of the absolute values of the two derivatives of its kind).
OpIsHelperInvocationEXT is the `helper_invocation` builtin, true for a lane that is a helper and for a lane that has been demoted.
OpDemoteToHelperInvocation becomes `demote`, which does not end the block.
Capabilities ShaderNonUniform, RuntimeDescriptorArray, SampledImageArrayNonUniformIndexing and DemoteToHelperInvocation and the NonUniform decoration are accepted.
Rectangle, buffer and subpass images, a multisampled image read other than by OpImageFetch, a fetch of a cube image, a gather of an image that is not 2D or cube, a texel type other than 32-bit float or integer, the MinLod operand, Grad on a cube image, a projective depth compare, derivatives outside a fragment shader or of other than f32, combined image samplers, and an image or sampler passed to a function or through a phi are refused by name.
A fragment shader's built-ins SampleId, SamplePosition, SampleMask (in and out), HelperInvocation, PointCoord, PrimitiveId, Layer, FragDepth and FragStencilRefEXT and a vertex shader's BaseVertex, BaseInstance, DrawIndex, Layer and ViewportIndex are lowered (the first group as `builtin` values, the outputs as stage outputs); capabilities Geometry, SampleRateShading, GroupNonUniform, ShaderLayer, ShaderViewportIndex and DrawParameters are accepted for them, and StencilExportEXT (with the StencilRefReplacingEXT mode) for FragStencilRefEXT, and MultiView for ViewIndex (a vertex shader's is lowered as a `builtin` the backend makes a constant; a fragment shader's is its layer).
ClipDistance, CullDistance, barycentrics and a fragment shader's ViewportIndex are refused by name: the hardware has none (valhall 14.3.7).
A struct or array loaded or stored whole is limited to 4096 leaves.
Volatile and Coherent decorations on variables are ignored; only a memory operand's Volatile counts.
`pow` is written as exp2(y * log2 x), so it is defined only where GLSL's is (x > 0).
